QPOSS was obtained by ring opening reaction of octaammonium POSS (OPOSS) with glycidyltrimethylammonium chloride (GDTMAC). The chemical structure of quaternized POSS (QPOSS) was confirmed by 1H NMR (Fig. 3
                        ) and FTIR (Fig. 4
                        ) spectral techniques. The peaks around 1.73 (Ha), 2.79 (Hb) and 3.4 ppm (Hc) are associated with the chemical shifts of protons from –CH2Si, - CH2- and –CH2N respectively (Fig. 3). The new peak appeared at around 4.02 ppm (Hd) in QPOSS correspond to the chemical shift of methyl protons of glycidyltrimethylammonium chloride (GTMAC), indicating successful quaternization of OPOSS and the extent of quaternization obtained was about 33.3% [13].

The long-term stability is another bottleneck of AEMs for electrochemical application, particularly under alkaline conditions due to the well-known degradation pathways of quaternary ammonium groups, including direct nucleophilic substitution, Hofmann elimination, and the formation of nitrogen ylide [30,45-47]. To evaluate the alkaline stability, membranes with different QPOSS content were immersed in 1 M, 2 M and 5 M KOH at different temperatures and the weight loss were analyzed. In addition, the change in hydroxide conductivity of the membranes after ageing in 1 M KOH was also monitored to assess the alkaline tolerance during prolonged exposure. The variation of hydroxide ion conductivity is recorded in Fig. 10
                           . No weight loss was noticed up to 7 days of continuous exposure in 1 M and 2 M KOH solutions at ambient temperature (25 °C) (Table S2). On the other hand, reduction in weight associated with the degradation of cationic domains noticed after 7 days of exposure in 5 M KOH solution at ambient temperature (Table S2) and different molar concentrations of alkaline solutions at 80 °C (Table 2
                           ). However, it is worth mentioning that the reduction was not much significant which is attributed to the formation of strong intermolecular interaction among the polymer and filler that improved the membrane alkaline stability.
